{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>Every test passed. Production was returning something else entirely.</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A component fetches a user's profile and renders <code>user.fullName</code>. Its test hardcodes a fake response: <code>{ fullName: "Jane Doe" }</code>. The test passes, every time, forever, right up until the backend team renames <code>fullName</code> to <code>displayName</code> in a real API change. Production breaks immediately, the test suite stays green, because the test was never actually checking against the real API, it was checking against a guess about the real API frozen in a hardcoded object.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A hand-written fixture is a snapshot of what someone believed the API returned on the day they wrote the test. Nothing connects that fixture to the real endpoint's actual current shape, so a backend change and a frontend fixture can drift apart indefinitely with zero signal, no failing test, no warning, until a real user hits it in production.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is a contract problem, not a testing-diligence problem: the frontend and backend each have their own belief about the response shape, and nothing forces those beliefs to reconcile. More tests, or more careful fixture-writing, doesn't fix this, it just adds more independent copies of the same possibly-wrong belief that could each individually be well-written and still collectively be stale.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Intercept the network, not the function call, and share the schema</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Mock Service Worker (MSW) doesn't replace your fetch code with a fake function, it intercepts the actual network request your app already makes, at the network layer, and returns a mocked response. Your component's real <code>fetch('/api/profile')</code> call runs exactly as it would in production, only the response is substituted, so nothing about the component's actual data-fetching code is bypassed or specially mocked.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The bigger win is pairing MSW's mocked responses with a schema (a Zod schema, an OpenAPI spec, a JSON Schema file) that's shared with, or validated against, the real backend's actual contract. Instead of a hand-typed <code>{ fullName: "Jane Doe" }</code> that nobody checks against reality, the mock response is generated from, or validated against, the same schema the real API is supposed to honor, so a schema mismatch is a build-time or test-time failure instead of a silent runtime one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This still requires the schema itself to be trustworthy, MSW plus an unvalidated hand-maintained schema is the same drift problem one level removed. The strongest version of this pattern generates the schema from something the backend actually enforces (an OpenAPI spec the backend validates its own responses against, a shared TypeScript types package published from the backend's source of truth), so the frontend's mock and the backend's real behavior are both downstream of one document, instead of being two independent guesses.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The date field that was a string until it wasn't</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's order-history component had tests mocking the orders API with hand-written fixtures like <code>{ orderId: "1", placedAt: "2024-01-15" }</code>. The backend later changed <code>placedAt</code> from a date string to an ISO timestamp with milliseconds and a timezone offset, a reasonable, well-documented change on their end. Every frontend test kept passing, they were still asserting against the old hardcoded string. The date-formatting logic that assumed a plain date string broke in production for every order placed after the deploy.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The team adopted MSW with response mocks generated from the backend's published OpenAPI schema rather than hand-typed per test. The next time the backend changed a field's shape, the generated mock changed with it automatically (the schema generation step failed loudly, flagging exactly which field's shape no longer matched what the frontend's TypeScript types expected), surfacing the break at build time, in CI, instead of after a real deploy.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The team also added a contract-test suite that runs the same MSW-backed component tests against the schema in CI on every backend PR that touches the OpenAPI spec, not just on frontend PRs, closing the gap where a backend-only change previously had zero automated signal on the frontend at all until someone happened to notice in staging.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

