{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The schema still has to be the real contract, not a second guess</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      MSW alone doesn't fix drift, it's the network-interception mechanism, not the source of truth. The actual fix is pairing it with a schema that's genuinely shared with (or generated from) the backend's real contract. MSW with a hand-maintained schema nobody validates against the backend is a smaller version of the same original problem, one layer removed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Where possible, generate frontend types and MSW mock schemas directly from the backend's own OpenAPI spec or a shared schema package, rather than hand-writing a frontend-side approximation of what the backend does. A generated schema can only be wrong if the backend's own spec is wrong, a hand-written one can be wrong for an unlimited number of independent human-error reasons.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Consider running the same MSW-backed contract tests in CI on backend PRs that touch the shared schema, not just frontend PRs, since a backend-only change is exactly the failure mode this pattern exists to catch, and it produces zero signal if only the frontend repository's CI ever runs it. For services without a formal OpenAPI spec, a lighter version still works: generate the schema from the backend's actual runtime response via a contract-testing tool (Pact and similar) that verifies both sides against a shared, versioned contract rather than trusting either side's independent belief.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

